WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.264
Day 29 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.814 --> 00:00:09.949
QA infra for AI: ephemeral runners, local models, secrets and cost.

00:00:10.499 --> 00:00:15.408
The five infrastructure decisions that keep AI testing reproducible, safe and

00:00:15.408 --> 00:00:16.109
affordable.

00:00:17.409 --> 00:00:22.469
The evals, agents and security tests from the last few days run on something:

00:00:22.469 --> 00:00:25.295
runners, containers, models, keys and data.

00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:28.059
That infrastructure is rarely designed.

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:33.170
It gets assembled from whatever is at hand, and one day the pipeline costs three

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:37.440
times as much, a result can't be reproduced or a key shows up in a log.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:43.909
Infrastructure decisions for QA with AI are few and have long consequences.

00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:47.172
Where the model runs defines cost and privacy.

00:00:47.472 --> 00:00:50.389
What runners are like defines reproducibility.

00:00:50.689 --> 00:00:54.511
Where an agent works defines how much damage a mistake can cause.

00:00:54.811 --> 00:00:59.371
This guide walks through those decisions with a practical rule for each, and with

00:00:59.371 --> 00:01:03.987
two metrics almost nobody publishes that are worth having from day one: tokens and

00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:04.775
spend per run.

00:01:06.075 --> 00:01:07.773
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:08.323 --> 00:01:10.288
The pipeline from day 28.

00:01:10.838 --> 00:01:12.071
Docker on the runner.

00:01:13.371 --> 00:01:14.651
This is the setup.

00:01:14.951 --> 00:01:19.168
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:20.468 --> 00:01:21.992
The five decisions.

00:01:22.542 --> 00:01:27.673
Where the model runs: cloud API (simple, pay per token, data leaves) or a local

00:01:27.673 --> 00:01:31.635
model on the runner (free, private, slower and less capable).

00:01:31.935 --> 00:01:36.245
My rule: local for smoke and format, cloud for judged evals.

00:01:36.795 --> 00:01:40.605
Runners: persistent or ephemeral (a clean container per run).

00:01:40.905 --> 00:01:43.242
My rule: always ephemeral.

00:01:43.792 --> 00:01:48.058
Where an agent works: on the runner with credentials or in a sandbox with no

00:01:48.058 --> 00:01:50.865
production credentials and limited network egress.

00:01:51.165 --> 00:01:53.095
My rule: sandbox.

00:01:53.645 --> 00:01:58.842
Secrets: one shared key or one per environment, with least privilege and a spending

00:01:58.842 --> 00:01:59.092
cap.

00:01:59.392 --> 00:02:01.438
My rule: per environment.

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:05.927
Data: a production copy or synthetic and anonymized.

00:02:06.227 --> 00:02:08.493
My rule: never production.

00:02:09.793 --> 00:02:10.829
Step 1.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:12.583
Where the model runs.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:15.702
Decide where the model runs in CI.

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:20.737
A local model as a service covers smoke and format checks at no cost; judged evals

00:02:20.737 --> 00:02:22.239
need a more capable model.

00:02:22.789 --> 00:02:26.704
There are two options, and the usual thing is to combine them.

00:02:27.004 --> 00:02:31.434
A local model, started as a service inside the runner, has no per-token cost and

00:02:31.434 --> 00:02:35.586
takes no data out of your infrastructure, but it's slower and less capable.

00:02:35.886 --> 00:02:41.195
A cloud API offers more capable models, charges per use and receives your data.

00:02:41.495 --> 00:02:46.139
The rule that works best is assigning each job to the cheapest model that solves it:

00:02:46.139 --> 00:02:50.727
smoke and format checks with the local model, judged evals with a more capable one.

00:02:51.027 --> 00:02:53.689
Measure before assuming the small one is enough.

00:02:54.989 --> 00:02:56.187
Step 2.

00:02:56.487 --> 00:02:57.906
Ephemeral runners.

00:02:58.456 --> 00:03:01.512
Use ephemeral runners: every run starts clean.

00:03:01.812 --> 00:03:06.066
Leftover state from a previous run is a frequent cause of results you can't

00:03:06.066 --> 00:03:06.633
reproduce.

00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:12.083
An ephemeral runner starts clean on every run: a new container, with no files,

00:03:12.083 --> 00:03:14.721
caches or processes from the previous run.

00:03:15.021 --> 00:03:19.882
Persistent runners accumulate state, such as a dependency installed by hand, a

00:03:19.882 --> 00:03:22.500
temporary file or a model someone updated.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.111
That state makes the same commit give different results depending on which machine

00:03:27.111 --> 00:03:27.795
it landed on.

00:03:28.095 --> 00:03:32.760
With models, which already bring their own variation, adding the environment's makes

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.704
it impossible to know what changed.

00:03:35.004 --> 00:03:38.211
Always ephemeral, with versions pinned in the image.

00:03:39.511 --> 00:03:40.593
Step 3.

00:03:40.893 --> 00:03:42.568
Sandbox for agents.

00:03:43.118 --> 00:03:48.252
If an agent executes commands in CI, have it do so in a sandbox: a container with no

00:03:48.252 --> 00:03:51.491
production credentials and restricted network egress.

00:03:52.041 --> 00:03:56.707
If an agent executes commands inside the pipeline, for example to repair a test or

00:03:56.707 --> 00:04:01.487
investigate a failure, it must do so in an isolated environment: a container with no

00:04:01.487 --> 00:04:05.982
production credentials, with the file system limited to the repository and with

00:04:05.982 --> 00:04:09.168
network egress restricted to the necessary destinations.

00:04:09.468 --> 00:04:14.167
The agent reads content that may carry malicious instructions, as we saw on day

00:04:14.167 --> 00:04:14.881
twenty-four.

00:04:15.181 --> 00:04:19.837
The sandbox guarantees that, if one of those instructions takes effect, nothing

00:04:19.837 --> 00:04:21.546
valuable is within its reach.

00:04:22.846 --> 00:04:23.859
Step 4.

00:04:24.159 --> 00:04:25.032
Secrets.

00:04:25.582 --> 00:04:30.602
Secrets: one API key per environment, with least privilege and a spending cap, and

00:04:30.602 --> 00:04:33.480
verify it doesn't show up in logs or artifacts.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:39.032
Use one API key per environment: one for development, one for CI and one for

00:04:39.032 --> 00:04:39.756
production.

00:04:40.056 --> 00:04:42.752
Each with least privilege and a spending cap.

00:04:43.052 --> 00:04:47.679
That way a leaked key or a runaway loop in CI affects neither production nor the

00:04:47.679 --> 00:04:48.546
overall budget.

00:04:48.846 --> 00:04:53.063
Store them in the CI system's secrets manager, never in the repository.

00:04:53.363 --> 00:04:58.167
And actively verify they don't show up in logs or artifacts, because some tools

00:04:58.167 --> 00:05:00.843
print the full configuration when they fail.

00:05:02.143 --> 00:05:03.272
Step 5.

00:05:03.572 --> 00:05:04.921
Cost as a metric.

00:05:05.471 --> 00:05:09.955
Cost as a metric: report tokens and spend per run next to the pass rate.

00:05:10.255 --> 00:05:12.173
What isn't measured grows.

00:05:12.723 --> 00:05:17.115
Publish tokens consumed and spend on every run, next to the pass rate.

00:05:17.415 --> 00:05:19.333
What isn't measured grows.

00:05:19.633 --> 00:05:24.421
A dataset that doubles, a judge that gets added or one more repetition are all

00:05:24.421 --> 00:05:26.509
reasonable changes nobody adds up.

00:05:26.809 --> 00:05:31.212
With the number in view, each change shows its cost and the team can decide whether

00:05:31.212 --> 00:05:31.954
it's worth it.

00:05:32.254 --> 00:05:37.027
It also lets you detect anomalies: a run costing ten times the usual tends to

00:05:37.027 --> 00:05:39.072
indicate an error, not more work.

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:41.559
Step 6.

00:05:41.859 --> 00:05:43.429
Reproducibility.

00:05:43.979 --> 00:05:49.085
Reproducibility: pinned tool versions (lockfile) and model versions, and results

00:05:49.085 --> 00:05:50.936
archived with their run card.

00:05:51.486 --> 00:05:53.787
Two things make a run reproducible.

00:05:54.087 --> 00:05:58.481
The first is pinning versions: tools through the lockfile and the container image,

00:05:58.481 --> 00:06:00.731
and models through their exact identifier.

00:06:01.031 --> 00:06:06.126
The second is archiving results with their run card, the one from day fifteen, for a

00:06:06.126 --> 00:06:07.036
defined period.

00:06:07.336 --> 00:06:11.834
When someone asks why a result changed between two dates, you'll be able to compare

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:15.953
which version of each thing was used on each, instead of trying to remember.

00:06:17.253 --> 00:06:18.359
An example.

00:06:18.909 --> 00:06:23.126
A team reviews its eval pipeline after a month with higher spending than planned.

00:06:23.426 --> 00:06:28.604
On publishing tokens per run, they discover the format check, which runs on every

00:06:28.604 --> 00:06:31.416
pull request, uses the most expensive model.

00:06:31.716 --> 00:06:34.993
They move it to a local model started as a service on the runner.

00:06:35.293 --> 00:06:39.696
Format is validated just the same, and that step stops costing anything.

00:06:39.996 --> 00:06:42.076
Judged evals stay in the cloud.

00:06:42.376 --> 00:06:46.346
They also find that two of their three runners were persistent and had different

00:06:46.346 --> 00:06:50.018
versions of a tool, which explained different results for the same commit.

00:06:50.318 --> 00:06:53.781
They replace them with ephemeral containers with a pinned image.

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.744
The most common mistakes.

00:06:57.294 --> 00:07:01.046
Using the most expensive model for checks a local one handles.

00:07:01.596 --> 00:07:04.455
Keeping persistent runners with accumulated state.

00:07:05.005 --> 00:07:08.363
Letting an agent run in CI with production credentials.

00:07:08.913 --> 00:07:12.097
Sharing a single API key across all environments.

00:07:13.397 --> 00:07:15.640
To know it is done, check this.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:20.059
Each type of eval has an assigned model, with its justification.

00:07:20.609 --> 00:07:23.677
Runners are ephemeral and the image pins versions.

00:07:24.227 --> 00:07:27.840
Agents run in a sandbox with no production credentials.

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:31.736
Each run publishes tokens and spend next to the result.

00:07:33.036 --> 00:07:34.931
And where AI fell short.

00:07:35.481 --> 00:07:39.513
A small local model is enough for smoke and format, not for judgment.

00:07:39.813 --> 00:07:43.635
Choosing a model on cost without measuring quality gets expensive.

00:07:44.935 --> 00:07:46.609
That was day 29.

00:07:46.909 --> 00:07:51.884
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:51.884 --> 00:07:52.891
asked questions.

00:07:53.441 --> 00:07:59.469
Continue with day 30: LLM observability in production: traces, online evals and

00:07:59.469 --> 00:08:00.003
alerts.
